\section{Introduction}
Aweer (also Aweera, Boni; ISO 639-3 \texttt{bob}, Glottocode awee1242) is a Cushitic language of eastern Kenya, written in the Latin alphabet. Search results report about 7,600 speakers (2009 census) and note that ``Aweer'' is the preferred name because ``Boni'' may be pejorative. Some speakers have shifted to Orma, Somali or Swahili. Only scholarly and background sources were found.

\section{Primary Educational Resources}
No primers, textbooks or school materials were found in the searches.

\section{Linguistic and Reference Materials}
\begin{itemize}
\item Heine, \emph{Boni Dialects} (1982), Glottolog record: \url{https://glottolog.org/resource/reference/id/19143}
\item Heine and M\"ohlig (1982), \emph{Language and Dialect Atlas of Kenya}, Glottolog record: \url{https://glottolog.org/resource/reference/id/57788}
\item Overview of the language: \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aweer_language}
\end{itemize}

\section{Religious and Community Resources}
None found in the searches.

\section{Digital and Audio Resources}
\begin{itemize}
\item Endangered Languages Project entry: \url{https://www.endangeredlanguages.com/lang/3948}
\end{itemize}
No audio, dictionary or standard orthography was found.

\section{Curriculum Development Context}
\begin{itemize}
\item Rilling, \emph{The Boni/Dahalo Report} (SIL, 1986), a sociolinguistic report, Glottolog record: \url{https://glottolog.org/resource/reference/id/112051}
\item Rilling (2012), electronic version, Glottolog record: \url{https://glottolog.org/resource/reference/id/301582}
\end{itemize}
The language is reported as not taught in schools. Curriculum work would need new materials developed with the community.
\end{document}
